{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage","id":1094247,"title":"Implications of increasing damage?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-05-18T11:45:31-07:00","id":1094247,"title":"Implications of increasing damage?","content":"What would the implications be, on a campaign-wide scale, of increasing the damage of all weapons by +2 across the board?<br /> <br /> My aim would be to make combat more lethal. Increasing damage would serve that purpose, and in it it makes combat something players would (hopefully) want to avoid if at all possible. When someone pulls a knife or a gun, I want the players (or the NPCs) to step back and consider the gravity of the situation. I think that gets lost sometimes. But are there other considerations that I am not thinking about?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T11:56:56-07:00","id":1094249,"title":"","content":"It disproportionately favors low damage weapons unless the damage increase is proportional to original damage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T12:07:42-07:00","id":1094254,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lnodiv</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1094249#post1094249\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It disproportionately favors low damage weapons unless the damage increase is proportional to original damage.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good point, Inodiv. I could increase all +0 damage to +1, then double everything else. If I do it this way instead of the flat +2, I ponder the same question/concern.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T12:21:54-07:00","id":1094259,"title":"","content":"It would make Tilts much easier to inflict, which may or may not be a problem if your goal is higher lethality. Ironically, this would be more easily mitigated if you increased all weapon damage by a fixed amount, because you could increase the damage thresholds for Tilts by the same amount.<br /> <br /> It also increases the impact of the dice roll. With increased damage values, it becomes easier to end a fight in one hit, which means more fights will be decided by a single lucky roll. In a similar vein, it decreases the importance of Defense and increases the importance of Health and Armor - Defense is all or nothing, and no matter how high you get your defense, your opponent will never have less than a 1/10 chance of hitting you, so you're better of investing in making sure you can survive the hit when it comes than in making yourself harder to hit.<br /> <br /> It also significantly increases the importance of Initiative. If you can count on both parties being able to take the other down in one hit, it becomes a game of whoever goes first wins.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 05-18-2017, 03:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T14:03:57-07:00","id":1094295,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1094259#post1094259\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It also significantly increases the importance of Initiative. If you can count on both parties being able to take the other down in one hit, it becomes a game of whoever goes first wins.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is the biggest mechanical issue. Increasing damage doesn't make combat scary if you can reliably use that increased damage to kill your opponents before they can kill you back. Then all the sudden you want to get into combat because you'll always win, so the threat of damage back at you is decreased.<br /> <br /> It also makes stealth characters even more preferable because ambushes are that much more deadly.<br /> <br /> ------<br /> <br /> In general, you can't discourage combat with things like this. The &quot;shock&quot; value only lasts a long as it takes for the players to attempt to the new paradigm. If you want to make combat something players don't like to consider (which I'm not convinced is a good goal to enforce mechanically), you need to make combat less certain, not more deadly. The harder it is to predict who will come out on top of a fight, the harder it is to feel safe you have enough of an edge to win.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T19:57:21-07:00","id":1094396,"title":"","content":"True that heavy arms","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13426-ameraaaaaa","name":"Ameraaaaaa","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T06:41:30-07:00","id":1094517,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1094295#post1094295\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is the biggest mechanical issue. Increasing damage doesn't make combat scary if you can reliably use that increased damage to kill your opponents before they can kill you back. Then all the sudden you want to get into combat because you'll always win, so the threat of damage back at you is decreased.<br /> <br /> It also makes stealth characters even more preferable because ambushes are that much more deadly.</div> </div> </div> </div>But isn't that the paradigm? Usually the one who draws first and fires usually (but not always, I understand) has the advantage? But I think it would be unwise assume that you would go first every time you get into combat. Now you bring up the subject of going first and I think that's where the focus would be: winning initiative would now become paramount.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1094295#post1094295\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In general, you can't discourage combat with things like this. The &quot;shock&quot; value only lasts a long as it takes for the players to attempt to the new paradigm. If you want to make combat something players don't like to consider (which I'm not convinced is a good goal to enforce mechanically), you need to make combat less certain, not more deadly. The harder it is to predict who will come out on top of a fight, the harder it is to feel safe you have enough of an edge to win.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good point! What would you suggest? Initiative every round?<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T06:48:52-07:00","id":1094524,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1094259#post1094259\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It would make Tilts much easier to inflict, which may or may not be a problem if your goal is higher lethality. Ironically, this would be more easily mitigated if you increased all weapon damage by a fixed amount, because you could increase the damage thresholds for Tilts by the same amount.<br /> <br /> It also increases the impact of the dice roll. With increased damage values, it becomes easier to end a fight in one hit, which means more fights will be decided by a single lucky roll. In a similar vein, it decreases the importance of Defense and increases the importance of Health and Armor - Defense is all or nothing, and no matter how high you get your defense, your opponent will never have less than a 1/10 chance of hitting you, so you're better of investing in making sure you can survive the hit when it comes than in making yourself harder to hit.<br /> <br /> It also significantly increases the importance of Initiative. If you can count on both parties being able to take the other down in one hit, it becomes a game of whoever goes first wins.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that was my thinking about Tilts, Charl.<br /> <br /> My objective is to try to (better?) stike a balance between playability and realism. Usually a person (in the real world) is lucky - or considered to be - when a single bullet <i>doesn't </i>kill them. I understand that's my opinion, and I understand the converse is true (as combat kind of is now) where many shot won't kill you. It just seems like the latter scenario is more likely as the rules stand. My thought is that, with a damage boost, a substantial attack roll (or a &quot;lucky roll&quot;) will do terrible damage, but still most likely not kill someone outright. <br /> <br /> But your point about armor does cause some concern.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T07:41:39-07:00","id":1094539,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Dynel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1094517#post1094517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But I think it would be unwise assume that you would go first every time you get into combat. Now you bring up the subject of going first and I think that's where the focus would be: winning initiative would now become paramount.<br /> <br /> Good point! What would you suggest? Initiative every round?</div> </div> </div> </div>This can be gamed by putting your Attributes into Initiative-boosting ones, Dexterity and Composure. Especially beneficial since weapons now do damage you might otherwise rely on Strength to bring.<br /> <br /> And almost every splat now has a way to jump to the top of the Initiative queue or otherwise gain another boost to it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 05-19-2017, 10:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T07:49:46-07:00","id":1094544,"title":"","content":"Yah instead just put your i guess humen players against were wolves in war form just 1 will do then they might face other supernaturals and for other humens just give them good guns and put 4 of them then just look at hurt locker","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13426-ameraaaaaa","name":"Ameraaaaaa","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T07:50:26-07:00","id":1094545,"title":"","content":"Then they will be afraid","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13426-ameraaaaaa","name":"Ameraaaaaa","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T08:55:36-07:00","id":1094576,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Dynel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1094517#post1094517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But isn't that the paradigm? Usually the one who draws first and fires usually (but not always, I understand) has the advantage? But I think it would be unwise assume that you would go first every time you get into combat. Now you bring up the subject of going first and I think that's where the focus would be: winning initiative would now become paramount.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I don't particularly like how it's handled, the game uses a weapons initiative penalty to off-set its damage value. So the bigger the weapon, the worse it is in on initiative, so there's a game balance (that's not great for people concerned with realism) that occurs. You can take a bit honking weapon that'll probably take most things out in 1-2 hits, but you'll very likely end up going last. You can easily jack up the odds of going first (not even getting into supernatural powers that automatically do that), but that's going to put you with weapons that aren't as likely to end the fight quickly.<br /> <br /> As well, the current 2e updates to defensive options make it a lot easier to survive someone trying to hit you hard before you can even go.<br /> <br /> Going first and hitting hard is still the general best option, but it's not a sure win on purpose.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Good point! What would you suggest? Initiative every round? </div> </div> </div>No. Random Init is more of a trap than it seems. The odds of who wins Init skew extremely quickly once you have a +2 or greater advantage you'll statistically win way more than you'll lose.<br /> <br /> Honestly, I don't really think there's much you can do in the system because I think the system is already pretty good for making combat not a great option due to the ease of getting in over your head (which is different from violence in general).<br /> <br /> I'd also note that in reality the deadliness of combat is really focused on one factor: how good and how fast you get medical attention. &quot;Instant&quot; kills are exceedingly rare. Most people that die of gunshots die over an hour later, not in the moment. Hell, 1/3 of people <b>impaled in the heart</b> survive it with medical treatment. Guns casually killing people with one or two hits is fantasy, not reality.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T09:34:41-07:00","id":1094592,"title":"","content":"Well said man","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13426-ameraaaaaa","name":"Ameraaaaaa","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T10:46:39-07:00","id":1094616,"title":"","content":"Really the only sure way to get players to take the threat of their character dying in combat seriously is to kill one, then make the player write up a new, starting character. After that every time he feels like the weak hitter on the roster will be a reminder to him and all his popcorn crunching buddies that combat really does come with negative consequences.<br /> \u200bIt's the unconscious belief in protagonist immortality that causes their cavalier attitudes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T11:06:37-07:00","id":1094620,"title":"","content":"Two words: Lasting Repercussions.<br /> <br /> Players will be much more reticent to leap into combat if a mistake stays with them longer than a few hours. Player gets shot, turn that into arm wrack and deny them an arm for a month. Player kills someone, slap them with a PTSD condition that rears its head whenever they come into later combats.<br /> <br /> Be wary about killing characters though. That sounds like a good option, but frequently has the opposite effect. If players are afraid of losing their character in combat they'll deliberately spec to be better at combat. Which, in turn, forces you to turn up combat lethality. This spirals upward until the system splinters under combat optimization.<br /> <br /> Consider also evaluating the goals of combat. Who are the players fighting? Why are they fighting? What will they gain from combat? A random group of mooks is just something to beat up for fun, but the beloved town sheriff mesmerized by vampiric magic is a tragic figure to save. Basically, the more deeply tied the action is to the characters and the plot the more likely they'll have reasons to avoid violence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-22T14:05:19-07:00","id":1095613,"title":"","content":"Maybe a variation of this Condition could help:<br /> <br /> Damaged (Persistent)<br /> <br /> You got a bad knee or shoulder from your sports days or the time that werewolf chewed you. After three combat turns, it's obvious that something's wrong with it. This Condition may reflect multiple body parts. Decrease the damage for inflicting a Tilt related to this parts by one. (Option 2: Receiving a successful specified target attack on a related body part causes an additional point of damage)<br /> <br /> Possible Sources: Getting a Tilt involving the body part, be on the receiving end of an exceptional success attack roll.<br /> <br /> Resolution: Gain a dot of Athletics, get it healed with physical therapy, surgery, magic ritual or other Storyteller approved mean.<br /> <br /> Beat: End an altercation without getting a Tilt related with this Condition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12173-basilistik","name":"Basilistik","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T07:33:58-07:00","id":1098760,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Basilistik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1095613#post1095613\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe a variation of this Condition could help:<br /> <br /> Damaged (Persistent)<br /> <br /> You got a bad knee or shoulder from your sports days or the time that werewolf chewed you. After three combat turns, it's obvious that something's wrong with it. This Condition may reflect multiple body parts. Decrease the damage for inflicting a Tilt related to this parts by one. (Option 2: Receiving a successful specified target attack on a related body part causes an additional point of damage)<br /> <br /> Possible Sources: Getting a Tilt involving the body part, be on the receiving end of an exceptional success attack roll.<br /> <br /> Resolution: Gain a dot of Athletics, get it healed with physical therapy, surgery, magic ritual or other Storyteller approved mean.<br /> <br /> Beat: End an altercation without getting a Tilt related with this Condition.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like it. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PenDragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1094620#post1094620\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Two words: Lasting Repercussions.<br /> <br /> Players will be much more reticent to leap into combat if a mistake stays with them longer than a few hours. Player gets shot, turn that into arm wrack and deny them an arm for a month. Player kills someone, slap them with a PTSD condition that rears its head whenever they come into later combats.<br /> <br /> Be wary about killing characters though. That sounds like a good option, but frequently has the opposite effect. If players are afraid of losing their character in combat they'll deliberately spec to be better at combat. Which, in turn, forces you to turn up combat lethality. This spirals upward until the system splinters under combat optimization.<br /> <br /> Consider also evaluating the goals of combat. Who are the players fighting? Why are they fighting? What will they gain from combat? A random group of mooks is just something to beat up for fun, but the beloved town sheriff mesmerized by vampiric magic is a tragic figure to save. Basically, the more deeply tied the action is to the characters and the plot the more likely they'll have reasons to avoid violence.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like the idea of lasting repercussions. I would think that mimics reality quite well. And though it may not seem obvious, my intention is not to kill characters easier. Whether or not they get into gun fights, my aim is to make the damage of bullets more respectable. That said, the gist of what I'm trying to accomplish is to make things a bit more &quot;realistic&quot; within the confines of a game. <br /> <br /> When I consider gun combat, I think about two true statements: <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>People can take multiple gunshots and survive.</li> <li>People can die from one gunshot wound.</li> </ol><br /> Of these two statements, #1 is represented in the rules, quite well actually (at least in my opinion). #2 is not. I'm trying to address #2 without the validity of #1...much.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T08:04:24-07:00","id":1098768,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Dynel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1098760#post1098760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When I consider gun combat, I think about two true statements:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>People can take multiple gunshots and survive.</li> <li>People can die from one gunshot wound.</li> </ol>Of these two statements, #1 is represented in the rules, quite well actually (at least in my opinion). #2 is not. I'm trying to address #2 without the validity of #1...much.</div> </div> </div> </div>Slap a Bleeding Tilt on them with the normal damage. Baring some medical care, they will bleed out and die, over time. With care, they are likely to live (baring Failure or Dramatic Failure on the Medicine roll).<br /> <br /> #1: multiple low damage hits.<br /> #2: one good hit that applies the Tilt, after which the target bleeds out and dies. Or, heck, X-again means they die outright from extreme damage.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":7212,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","user":"Vent0","date":". 05-31-2017, 11:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T09:35:53-07:00","id":1098798,"title":"","content":"Hurt Locker covers making Tilts persistant after combat. I suggest checking that out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T09:38:32-07:00","id":1098800,"title":"","content":"Can you elaborate on how #2 isn't represented in the rules?<br /> <br /> A 2 success hit with a rifle is a fatal wound (without medical treatment) to unarmored Health 7 or lower characters (aka most humans). Even a character rolling a chance die will do that 1/100 times.<br /> <br /> There's always a chance an attack can kill a target. A chance die attack the does bashing damage still has the potentially to re-roll 10s enough to kill someone. Even without things like the Bleeding tilt option, all guns can kill a target in one shot with a good/lucky enough roll.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T10:01:12-07:00","id":1098809,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1098768#post1098768\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Slap a Bleeding Tilt on them with the normal damage. Baring some medical care, they will bleed out and die, over time. With care, they are likely to live (baring Failure or Dramatic Failure on the Medicine roll).<br /> <br /> #1: multiple low damage hits.<br /> #2: one good hit that applies the Tilt, after which the target bleeds out and dies. Or, heck, X-again means they die outright from extreme damage.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1098798#post1098798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hurt Locker covers making Tilts persistant after combat. I suggest checking that out.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, the Bleeding tilt sounds good. I'm still digesting Hurt Locker, so that's something I need to remember!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1098800#post1098800\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can you elaborate on how #2 isn't represented in the rules?<br /> <br /> A 2 success hit with a rifle is a fatal wound (without medical treatment) to unarmored Health 7 or lower characters (aka most humans). Even a character rolling a chance die will do that 1/100 times.<br /> <br /> There's always a chance an attack can kill a target. A chance die attack the does bashing damage still has the potentially to re-roll 10s enough to kill someone. Even without things like the Bleeding tilt option, all guns can kill a target in one shot with a good/lucky enough roll.</div> </div> </div> </div>I did say it wasn't represented in the rules. My original text was &quot;#2 is not...very well.&quot; That being said, I might amend my original statement to consider Tilts more in<br /> <br /> Addressing your example, Heavy Arms; a 2 success hit with a rifle only does 6 damage, which doesn't satisfy my #2 statement. If we're talking strictly damage, of course. If you take Tilts into consideration, it probably will be fatal. But more importantly, I don't get a warm and fuzzy feeling about needing a particular condition (a rifle and 2 successes) to have a fatal gunshot wound.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T10:17:08-07:00","id":1098812,"title":"","content":"A rifle with <b>3</b> successes, though, immediately drops the average person to dying.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T13:59:04-07:00","id":1098883,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Dynel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1098809#post1098809\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My original text was &quot;#2 is not...very well.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>OK, so besides my somewhat botched example, can still unpack this?<br /> <br /> What is, &quot;not very well,&quot; for you in this regard? Why is a few successes to outright KO and leave dying without medical treatment not sufficient to have guns be sufficiently deadly?<br /> <br /> You're making it sound like it's hard to get more than 1 success to be doing a lot of damage. Which is true for low-trained characters sure, but a character invested heavily into shooting people will have no problem getting one hit KOs that leave the target bleeding out on a regular basis.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T19:11:10-07:00","id":1098979,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1098883#post1098883\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> OK, so besides my somewhat botched example, can still unpack this?<br /> <br /> What is, &amp;quot;not very well,&amp;quot; for you in this regard? Why is a few successes to outright KO and leave dying without medical treatment not sufficient to have guns be sufficiently deadly?<br /> <br /> You're making it sound like it's hard to get more than 1 success to be doing a lot of damage. Which is true for low-trained characters sure, but a character invested heavily into shooting people will have no problem getting one hit KOs that leave the target bleeding out on a regular basis.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Guns are deadly. There's no argument there. But I'm not enthusiastic about the idea that there needs to be certain conditions to make them deadly (x amount of successes and an assault rifle, &quot;a few successes&quot; to kill actually kill someone, needing a character that's heavily invested in shooting, etc.). The original intent behind my idea to increase damage was to make the danger of gun combat better parallel reality. I know, I know...it's a game. I understand that. But it was still my intent.<br /> <br /> The statistics of the mortality rate from a gunshot wound jump around, depending on where you look, from 18 to 30 percent. Let's say that the mortality rate is 25 percent. That's one in four. If I make four attempts to kill someone (four separate attack rolls of average competency) with a gun, will one of those result in a kill (assuming 7-8 health)? Do I need to apply conditions such as &quot;assault rifle&quot; or &quot;a character heavily invested into shooting people&quot; to achieve that result? <br /> <br /> Not everyone dies from a well-trained shooter wielding an assault rifle. And I don't think they should in CoD, either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T19:39:57-07:00","id":1098996,"title":"","content":"It sounds like you want damage to be a bit more random and weighted toward higher numbers (fatal or near fatal damage) being easier to achieve. I think probably the best way to do that without breaking the system is to give more weapons 9 or even 8 again. That increases the average number of successes 10-20%, and makes a successful roll scarier because of the increased chance of cascading successes, without making total whiffs any less likely or removing the possibility of flesh wounds. <br /> <br /> You could also pair it with another hack I've considered, which is having weapon modifiers match successes, up to their maximum, instead of always granting the full modifier to any successful hit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T23:52:25-07:00","id":1099041,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Dynel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1098979#post1098979\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Guns are deadly. There's no argument there. But I'm not enthusiastic about the idea that there needs to be certain conditions to make them deadly (x amount of successes and an assault rifle, &quot;a few successes&quot; to kill actually kill someone, needing a character that's heavily invested in shooting, etc.). The original intent behind my idea to increase damage was to make the danger of gun combat better parallel reality. I know, I know...it's a game. I understand that. But it was still my intent.<br /> <br /> The statistics of the mortality rate from a gunshot wound jump around, depending on where you look, from 18 to 30 percent. Let's say that the mortality rate is 25 percent. That's one in four. If I make four attempts to kill someone (four separate attack rolls of average competency) with a gun, will one of those result in a kill (assuming 7-8 health) Do I need to apply conditions such as &quot;assault rifle&quot; or &quot;a character heavily invested into shooting people&quot; to achieve that result? <br /> <br /> Not everyone dies from a well-trained shooter wielding an assault rifle. And I don't think they should in CoD, either.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, that 18 to 30 percent is mortality for people who actually get hit by the bullet, which isn't a guarantee with an attack roll. So what you should be asking is if an average of one in four <i>successful hits</i> result in death for a character with 7 undamaged health boxes. Now, the answer is going to vary depending on the dice pool of the shooter and the gun they are shooting. Since you're asking about an average dice pool, let's go ahead and assume a base dice pool of 3: 2 from having average Dexterity and 1 from amature-level Skill with Firearms. With only 3 dice, the shooter is most likely only going to get 1 success. However, if they spend Willpower, their dice pool jumps up to 6, and if they fire a long burst or aim for 3 turns, it jumps up to a 9. With a light pistol, the shooter has a 2.1% chance of doing 7 damage on that dice pool before 10-again. A heavy pistol has roughly a 7.35% chance before 10-again. A shotgun has a 17.5% chance before 9-again. Most rifles have a 26.8% chance before 10-again.<br /> <br /> Of course, all of this is assuming we're trying to do all of the damage to kill the target with the gunshot alone. Which is kind of silly. Instant death from a gunshot is extremely rare, and it's usually the blood loss that kills people who get shot. The bleeding Tilt kicks in on <i>any successful hit</i> with <i>any firearm</i> on a target with 2 Stamina and no armor. Which <i>will</i> kill the victim if they don't get medical attention.<br /> <br /> Personally, I would argue that this is a reasonably accurate simulation of gun lethality - someone who's well-trained and/or <i>really</i> trying to kill you has a pretty decent chance of doing it in one shot if they use a big enough gun, and can very easily do it if they shoot you more than once. A single gunshot from an untrained shooter who's not making the maximum effort to be as lethal as possible probably won't kill you right away short of a stroke of exceptional luck, but will kill you eventually if you don't get medical attention in time. Sounds exactly like the way it should work to me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 06-01-2017, 02:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T01:21:35-07:00","id":1099057,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Dynel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1098979#post1098979\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Guns are deadly. There's no argument there.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, there is to an extent, because of how you want to define &quot;deadly&quot; here.<br /> <br /> Guns aren't that deadly under the aegis of, &quot;one shot one kill.&quot; There's a reason why that's the mantra of highly skilled marksmen. Guns are deadly because of volume of fire, ease of training, range, and difficulty to treat medically. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But I'm not enthusiastic about the idea that there needs to be certain conditions to make them deadly (x amount of successes and an assault rifle, &quot;a few successes&quot; to kill actually kill someone, needing a character that's heavily invested in shooting, etc.). </div> </div> </div>Why? This is kind of a meaningless statement. &quot;Being loaded&quot; could be a certain condition to make them deadly.<br /> <br /> Needing multiple successes is also how the combat system works. The game isn't built around one successes = win for combat. Even Down and Dirty combat, which is far deadlier if you want it to be (if your intent is to kill, winning in Down and Dirty is an auto kill) requires rolling more successes than your opponent.<br /> <br /> Someone heavily invested in shooting taking down characters more reliably than someone with less training isn't a condition, it's how things work.<br /> <br /> A person with minimal training isn't going to be dropping enemies like flies. You don't pick up a gun and suddenly know how to aim it in combat, etc. A trained combatant is a more effective killer.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The original intent behind my idea to increase damage was to make the danger of gun combat better parallel reality. </div> </div> </div>But it doesn't actually represent reality. Turning a rifle into a 6 damage weapon, means it's ridiculously deadly compared to reality.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The statistics of the mortality rate from a gunshot wound jump around, depending on where you look, from 18 to 30 percent. Let's say that the mortality rate is 25 percent. That's one in four. If I make four attempts to kill someone (four separate attack rolls of average competency) with a gun, will one of those result in a kill (assuming 7-8 health)? </div> </div> </div>That's not how mortality rates work.<br /> <br /> A 25% mortality rate covers all gun deaths, from one shot one kill, to shot twenty times and dies a week later.<br /> <br /> That's not &quot;guns should have a 25% chance to kill per attack,&quot; but a &quot;guns should have a 25% chance to kill per combat.&quot;<br /> <br /> This is were I see a massive disconnect with your desire for more reality. Mortality statistics treat having to shoot someone five times the same as shooting them once.<br /> <br /> So the demand for it to be more likely to do 7-8 damage has <b>nothing</b> to do with reality.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T03:15:45-07:00","id":1099090,"title":"","content":"indeed man indeed","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13426-ameraaaaaa","name":"Ameraaaaaa","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T04:17:47-07:00","id":1099108,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099057#post1099057\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, there is to an extent, because of how you want to define &quot;deadly&quot; here.</div> </div> </div> </div>First off, let me say that I hope to keep this conversation civil. I think gun combat is less lethal than I feel it should be. That is my opinion. I get the feeling that you're attempting to trying to come into this thread and tell me how &quot;wrong&quot; I am and strong-arm me out of my opinion. Sorry, that won't work. If I'm wrong about that, I apologize. But, if that's your intent, I'd appreciate it if you'd not do that. I certainly appreciate your opinion in return, and some posters have had some good points (I really liked the idea of being more liberal with Tilts and I'll honestly probably go that route instead of increasing damage) but if you're intent is to just try to talk over me by telling me I'm wrong, then that's a waste of your time. And I'd hate for you to waste your time. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Guns aren't that deadly under the aegis of, &quot;one shot one kill.&quot; There's a reason why that's the mantra of highly skilled marksmen. Guns are deadly because of volume of fire, ease of training, range, and difficulty to treat medically. </div> </div> </div>Gang members and cops are going to have a gun skill in the 3-4 range (according to the rulebook). That's pretty good skill. Using a .45, they might manage on average (without willpower) 3-4 damage, and maybe 4-5 damage with willpower. However, neither one will kill someone outright. Now factor in kids who play with guns, cheated-on wives, or the old guy who's protecting his home who also kill people with a gunshot wound. Now with the Bleeding Tilit (thanks Vent0 and others!) maybe the 4-5 damage goes over Stamina and the victim will die in a few rounds. Then, technically, that one wound <b>does</b> kill them. But there are occasions when a person doesn't get the chance to survive by staunching the blood flow - sometimes that one shot does them in. Period. My point is that these examples are real-life examples where a single bullet does kill a person. Hurt Locker goes a long way towards achieving that and, admittedly, might take care of my concerns. So maybe the conversation is academic at this point? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why? This is kind of a meaningless statement. &quot;Being loaded&quot; could be a certain condition to make them deadly.<br /> <br /> Needing multiple successes is also how the combat system works. The game isn't built around one successes = win for combat. Even Down and Dirty combat, which is far deadlier if you want it to be (if your intent is to kill, winning in Down and Dirty is an auto kill) requires rolling more successes than your opponent.<br /> <br /> Someone heavily invested in shooting taking down characters more reliably than someone with less training isn't a condition, it's how things work.<br /> <br /> A person with minimal training isn't going to be dropping enemies like flies. You don't pick up a gun and suddenly know how to aim it in combat, etc. A trained combatant is a more effective killer. </div> </div> </div>I'm okay with multiple successes needed, and I understand that's how the game works. I also understand one success does not equal &quot;win.&quot; And I get that a well-trained person has a better chance of killing someone in a less shots (or one shot) by achieving more successes. But, in game terms, that is a condition (at least in my opinion). The combat rules suggest that a single bullet won't kill someone unless it's a bigger gun in the hands of a trained person. That's not representative of reality (and again, I understand that it's a game). Your point here are valid - a more skilled person with heavier gun will get more successes and thus kill someone easier - but the converse (less skilled person with a handgun) is difficult-to-impossible under the current rules. That's the crux of my opinion - a less skilled person wielding a .38 can kill someone with a single shot. Not very often, granted, but can. The rules do not reflect this, in my opinion. We can dress it up or apply conditions all day long but I don't think the rules allow for it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But it doesn't actually represent reality. Turning a rifle into a 6 damage weapon, means it's ridiculously deadly compared to reality. </div> </div> </div>I agree with that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's not how mortality rates work.<br /> <br /> A 25% mortality rate covers all gun deaths, from one shot one kill, to shot twenty times and dies a week later.<br /> <br /> That's not &quot;guns should have a 25% chance to kill per attack,&quot; but a &quot;guns should have a 25% chance to kill per combat.&quot;<br /> <br /> This is were I see a massive disconnect with your desire for more reality. Mortality statistics treat having to shoot someone five times the same as shooting them once.<br /> <br /> So the demand for it to be more likely to do 7-8 damage has <b>nothing</b> to do with reality. </div> </div> </div>From what I could find, the mortality rates for a handgun wound is about 18% (sources vary from 15-20%). <i>A handgun</i>. And no, my research suggests a single wound. Hell, let's say the mortality rate is only 10% from getting shot by a .45. So, in the hands of an average shooter (4-5 dice pool), there's still a very little chance that one gunshot will kill a person, which I (again) feel isn't representative of reality.<br /> <br /> So I guess we're agreeing to disagree - there's no &quot;demand&quot; for a gun to do 7-8 damage, per se; the reality, though, is that one shot from a handgun can - <b>and does</b> - kill a person. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> But, HA (if I may call you that), this conversation has got me thinking about one aspect that I might not have considered. Maybe those who die from one gunshot wound are extremely <i>unlucky</i>; the shooter achieved multiple successes, inflicting considerable damage, and the person dies from blood loss and shock.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12124,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","user":"Lord Dynel","date":".  spelling errors 06-01-2017, 07:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T04:32:53-07:00","id":1099111,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099041#post1099041\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, that 18 to 30 percent is mortality for people who actually get hit by the bullet, which isn't a guarantee with an attack roll. So what you should be asking is if an average of one in four <i>successful hits</i> result in death for a character with 7 undamaged health boxes. Now, the answer is going to vary depending on the dice pool of the shooter and the gun they are shooting. Since you're asking about an average dice pool, let's go ahead and assume a base dice pool of 3: 2 from having average Dexterity and 1 from amature-level Skill with Firearms. With only 3 dice, the shooter is most likely only going to get 1 success. However, if they spend Willpower, their dice pool jumps up to 6, and if they fire a long burst or aim for 3 turns, it jumps up to a 9. With a light pistol, the shooter has a 2.1% chance of doing 7 damage on that dice pool before 10-again. A heavy pistol has roughly a 7.35% chance before 10-again. A shotgun has a 17.5% chance before 9-again. Most rifles have a 26.8% chance before 10-again.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I am strictly referring to a person who gets hit with a bullet. When, and if, the person gets hit, then the mortality rate is somewhere in the 18-30% range. I appreciate you examples, by the way! They certainly fall in line with my opinion of gun lethality.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course, all of this is assuming we're trying to do all of the damage to kill the target with the gunshot alone. Which is kind of silly. Instant death from a gunshot is extremely rare, and it's usually the blood loss that kills people who get shot. The bleeding Tilt kicks in on <i>any successful hit</i> with <i>any firearm</i> on a target with 2 Stamina and no armor. Which <i>will</i> kill the victim if they don't get medical attention. </div> </div> </div>The more I think about this, the more I am starting to see it. As I mentioned in my last post, maybe it's more academic at this point. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I think the Bleeding Tilt and Lasting Trauma really goes a long way towards replicating a more realistic and deadly nature of gun play. <br /> <br /> Personally, I would argue that this is a reasonably accurate simulation of gun lethality - someone who's well-trained and/or <i>really</i> trying to kill you has a pretty decent chance of doing it in one shot if they use a big enough gun, and can very easily do it if they shoot you more than once. A single gunshot from an untrained shooter who's not making the maximum effort to be as lethal as possible probably won't kill you right away short of a stroke of exceptional luck, but will kill you eventually if you don't get medical attention in time. Sounds exactly like the way it should work to me.[/QUOTE]<br /> <br /> Maybe one-shot kills are more contingent on the luck of the shooter...or the &quot;unluck&quot; of the victim. I'm not sure if I like the explanation though, but I suppose it's more reflective of real world conditions.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T04:55:58-07:00","id":1099116,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Dynel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099108#post1099108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> That's the crux of my opinion - a less skilled person wielding a .38 can kill someone with a single shot. Not very often, granted, but can. The rules do not reflect this, in my opinion. We can dress it up or apply conditions all day long but I don't think the rules allow for it.</div> </div> </div> </div> If you apply a Tilt / Condition that reflects bleeding out, how is this NOT deadly?<br /> <br /> There's a Bleeding Tilt in Hurt Locker. On an average person (2 Dex, 1 firearms, wp as suggested), using a gun, an average roll is enough to inflict the Bleeding Tilt on an average victim. She is taking one point of damage per turn on top of what she already suffered from being. That's four rounds (ten seconds) to unconciousness, twelve rounds to death, without the most basic medical treatment. Couple that with the shock of being, well, shot. Stunned, insensate. And likely still being shot at. And suffering wound penalties, making it harder to think of what to do? Its possible that your average person (2 strength) will be unable to apply pressure to themselves, let alone be able to fight back, so they can't really treat themselves effectively in this case.<br /> <br /> Werewolves can heal themselves, so its unlikely they will bleed out (and vampires/mummies can't), but a single, simple changeling? Definitely. A demon without Just Bruised? Yes. Even a Beast without an appropriate Atavism will die from a gunshot wound. Fairly sure Prometheans could bleed out too without electricity to heal. A Sin-Eater that doesn't Bulwark the damage? Most likely, depending on 2e rules. We touched on mortals / hunters. Mages could Pattern Restore, but that takes mana and your action away from, well, surviving gunshots being aimed their way; for a mage, if you don't have mage armor up, guns are still very scary. <br /> <br /> For the overwhelming majority of people, let alone <i>monsters</i>, being shot is still a big deal with just that one tilt.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 06-01-2017, 08:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T05:43:52-07:00","id":1099126,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099116#post1099116\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> If you apply a Tilt / Condition that reflects bleeding out, how is this NOT deadly?<br /> <br /> There's a Bleeding Tilt in Hurt Locker. On an average person (2 Dex, 1 firearms, wp as suggested), using a gun, an average roll is enough to inflict the Bleeding Tilt on an average victim. She is taking one point of damage per turn on top of what she already suffered from being. That's four rounds (ten seconds) to unconciousness, twelve rounds to death, without the most basic medical treatment. Couple that with the shock of being, well, shot. Stunned, insensate. And likely still being shot at. And suffering wound penalties, making it harder to think of what to do? Its possible that your average person (2 strength) will be unable to apply pressure to themselves, let alone be able to fight back, so they can't really treat themselves effectively in this case.<br /> <br /> Werewolves can heal themselves, so its unlikely they will bleed out (and vampires/mummies can't), but a single, simple changeling? Definitely. A demon without Just Bruised? Yes. Even a Beast without an appropriate Atavism will die from a gunshot wound. Fairly sure Prometheans could bleed out too without electricity to heal. A Sin-Eater that doesn't Bulwark the damage? Most likely, depending on 2e rules. We touched on mortals / hunters. Mages could Pattern Restore, but that takes mana and your action away from, well, surviving gunshots being aimed their way; for a mage, if you don't have mage armor up, guns are still very scary.<br /> <br /> For the overwhelming majority of people, let alone <i>monsters</i>, being shot is still a big deal with just that one tilt.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, I agree. Ultimately, I will probably go the Tilt/Lasting Trauma/Persistant Condition over increasing damage. If I do increase damage, it'll probably only be a +1 (either for all weapons across the board or, more likely, just for guns). The point I was making about applying conditions or dressing up the example/scenario (that you quoted) wasn't meant to mean a &quot;Condition&quot; in the form of a Tilt/Lasting Trauma/Persistant Condition. It was referring to applying special circumstances or conditions (little &quot;c&quot;) to the example to make my opinion/concern null and void...for example, if someone retorted &quot;a highly trained gunman can kill someone in one shot with a rifle, so what the hell are you talking about?&quot; kind of reply. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T06:24:08-07:00","id":1099130,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Dynel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099126#post1099126\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh, I agree. Ultimately, I will probably go the Tilt/Lasting Trauma/Persistant Condition over increasing damage. If I do increase damage, it'll probably only be a +1 (either for all weapons across the board or, more likely, just for guns). The point I was making about applying conditions or dressing up the example/scenario (that you quoted) wasn't meant to mean a &amp;quot;Condition&amp;quot; in the form of a Tilt/Lasting Trauma/Persistant Condition. It was referring to applying special circumstances or conditions (little &amp;quot;c&amp;quot<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> to the example to make my opinion/concern null and void...for example, if someone retorted &amp;quot;a highly trained gunman can kill someone in one shot with a rifle, so what the hell are you talking about?&amp;quot; kind of reply. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>You can also be liberal with called shot bonuses and effects. If targeting someone's head or heart deals bonus damage (instead of or in addition to Stun Tilts), an accidental shot might get the same, if you rule it lands there.<br /> <br /> Which might be another factor for gun lethality - were those Real Life examples making targeted shots for &quot;more damage&quot;?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T07:46:41-07:00","id":1099150,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099130#post1099130\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You can also be liberal with called shot bonuses and effects. If targeting someone's head or heart deals bonus damage (instead of or in addition to Stun Tilts), an accidental shot might get the same, if you rule it lands there.<br /> <br /> Which might be another factor for gun lethality - were those Real Life examples making targeted shots for &amp;quot;more damage&amp;quot;?</div> </div> </div> </div>The issue with called shots doing extra damage is a gameplay one. Because the choice between a less damaging general shot and a more damaging called shot is never a meaningful one. You can always do the math to figure out which has the higher expected damage value. It's not a tactical decision, it's just a trap for players who don't know the math.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T10:41:14-07:00","id":1099216,"title":"","content":"Called shots already do lots through Combat Tilts as well. Aim at an arm / leg / head, inflict Arm/Leg wreck / blind / deaf / stunned / knocked down.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T11:10:50-07:00","id":1099219,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099216#post1099216\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Called shots already do lots through Combat Tilts as well. Aim at an arm / leg / head, inflict Arm/Leg wreck / blind / deaf / stunned / knocked down.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, and those are meaningful choices because you can't objectively assess which option is superior.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T13:24:03-07:00","id":1099262,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099150#post1099150\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The issue with called shots doing extra damage is a gameplay one. Because the choice between a less damaging general shot and a more damaging called shot is never a meaningful one. You can always do the math to figure out which has the higher expected damage value. It's not a tactical decision, it's just a trap for players who don't know the math.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Would you mind elaborating on this thought?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T14:12:10-07:00","id":1099284,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Dynel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099262#post1099262\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Would you mind elaborating on this thought?</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure. So, say you have a dice pool of 9 with your light pistol, going with the earlier example. The math of expected successes in CofD's dice pool system is a little complicated, but for simplicity's sake we'll use the general rule that you can expect 1/3 of your dice pool in successes on average. So with no additional penalties, you can expect to do <i>around</i> 4 damage per shot with that dice pool. However, if you can make a called shot to increase your damage - let's say, for example, your opponent is wearing Sports Gear - 2/0 armor that covers the torso and arms. Targeting the head for a -3 penalty is always worth it. You give up 1 expected success for 2 damage on a hit, for a net gain of +1 to your expected damage value. If you choose to not make the called shot, you are falling into a trap choice because numerically speaking, it is strictly worse than taking the called shot.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, if your dice pool is 5, taking the called shot is the trap option, because the -3 penalty will take your expected number of successes below 1, and the extra 2 damage from getting around the armor only matters if you get at least one success on the dice roll.<br /> <br /> EDIT: of course, all of this assumes extra damage is the only difference between a called shot and a not-called shot. Having Tilts apply on called shots solves this problem because rather than trading chance to hit (and therefore damage) for more damage, which has an objectively best option, you're trading chance to hit for a special effect, the value of which is situational. That makes it a choice instead of a math test.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 06-01-2017, 05:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T14:32:34-07:00","id":1099290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099219#post1099219\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Indeed, and those are meaningful choices because you can't objectively assess which option is superior.</div> </div> </div> </div> Eyes. Blinding is always the superior option. #VentrueHate<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T14:35:08-07:00","id":1099291,"title":"","content":"Unless you only get one shot. Without the weight of averages behind it, it is no longer objectively better, but now a gamble.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T14:35:55-07:00","id":1099293,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099291#post1099291\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unless you only get one shot. Without the weight of averages behind it, it is no longer objectively better, but now a gamble.</div> </div> </div> </div>True that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T15:13:38-07:00","id":1099303,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Dynel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099108#post1099108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think gun combat is less lethal than I feel it should be. That is my opinion. I get the feeling that you're attempting to trying to come into this thread and tell me how &quot;wrong&quot; I am and strong-arm me out of my opinion. Sorry, that won't work. If I'm wrong about that, I apologize. But, if that's your intent, I'd appreciate it if you'd not do that.</div> </div> </div> </div>My intent here is to pull apart the desire for guns to be more lethal, and the stated desire to better reflect reality, because to me these are goals that have become in conflict.<br /> <br /> If you just want more lethal guns and ignore appeals to realism, that's a very different thing than saying that you want gun combat to better reflect reality by making it more lethal.<br /> <br /> You seem to have found your happy place with being liberal with the Bleeding Tilt in this regard, but a lot of my more stringent objections are about how you're portraying the reality of violence (as in the real world) rather than how one chooses to abstract that into the game; which I don't feel is a simple matter of opinions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Gang members and cops are going to have a gun skill in the 3-4 range (according to the rulebook). That's pretty good skill. Using a .45, they might manage on average (without willpower) 3-4 damage, and maybe 4-5 damage with willpower. However, neither one will kill someone outright. </div> </div> </div>This is a rather frustrating statement. The average damage is not a cap on damage. Averaging 3-5 damage does not mean you will not roll more than 5 total damage ever. It's an average, rolls will perform above and below average. A character with 4 dice, spending a WP, and using a 2 damage gun, has a &gt;7.5% chance to do 7 <b>or more</b> damage. That means they'll be dealing lethal attacks against average Health characters more frequently than you'd roll a natural 20 in D&amp;D. That means if five cops are having a shoot out with five gang members who all have these stats, there's ~83% one of them dies (or at least gets KOed and is dying) the first round of shooting (and the second round is going to get really messy with cumulative damage building up).<br /> <br /> So please, be careful with how to look at these numbers. Something like a 5% chance of a one-hit kill might feel very small per attack, but in a sub-system like combat with a lot of rolls, a 5% odds event will statistically come up fairly frequently even though it is such an outlier on an individual roll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now factor in kids who play with guns, cheated-on wives, or the old guy who's protecting his home who also kill people with a gunshot wound. </div> </div> </div>How many of those situations should be full combat instead of Down and Dirty combat? There's a reason the game doesn't force all violence into the full combat system. Sometimes that's not the best subsystem for the situation. How many of these should even be rolled? The CofD systems are expected to be selected as what's most appropriate to the situation.<br /> <br /> Why would I ever roll an NPC child shooting another NPC child while playing around with an unsecured gun?<br /> <br /> Why would I treat a spouse killing a cheating partner by surprise as a full combat situation instead of a Down and Dirty combat situation if it seems important enough to roll out? Either they kill their partner or they don't. That's the drama of the situation.<br /> <br /> The combat rules are not the &quot;physics&quot; of the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But there are occasions when a person doesn't get the chance to survive by staunching the blood flow - sometimes that one shot does them in. Period. My point is that these examples are real-life examples where a single bullet does kill a person. </div> </div> </div>Yes. The point here though, is that these are in reality rather rare, not that they don't happen, and in the rules-as-is, this is reflected by having outright kills be mathematically rare compared to multiple attack kills or mortal wounds that kill over time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So maybe the conversation is academic at this point? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> </div> </div> </div>Probably... I like academic conversations, so I keep going at them without really worrying about them being such.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The rules do not reflect this, in my opinion. We can dress it up or apply conditions all day long but I don't think the rules allow for it. </div> </div> </div>But this is what I was trying to get at. What makes you say this? Because the rules do allow for it. With potentially infinitely exploding dice, no number of successes on a roll is impossible, just growingly improbable. As well, the rules do not force all violence into the full combat system to mitigate some of the difficulty of the combat system with dramatic situations that aren't focusing on duking it out. How does &quot;unlikely&quot; get to &quot;impossible&quot; for you in this?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But, HA (if I may call you that), this conversation has got me thinking about one aspect that I might not have considered. Maybe those who die from one gunshot wound are extremely <i>unlucky</i>; the shooter achieved multiple successes, inflicting considerable damage, and the person dies from blood loss and shock. </div> </div> </div>I don't mind HA at all.<br /> <br /> And yeah... this is kind of what I was pushing for even if it didn't quite work as I intended.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T15:29:17-07:00","id":1099306,"title":"","content":"Or you're dealing with the kind of being whose other senses compensate, making the loss of eyes a minimal and temporary problem. Good choice with eye-based mind controllers, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T18:33:12-07:00","id":1099705,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099303#post1099303\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My intent here is to pull apart the desire for guns to be more lethal, and the stated desire to better reflect reality, because to me these are goals that have become in conflict.<br /> <br /> If you just want more lethal guns and ignore appeals to realism, that's a very different thing than saying that you want gun combat to better reflect reality by making it more lethal.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well...a more lethal gun would certainly appeal to realism... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You seem to have found your happy place with being liberal with the Bleeding Tilt in this regard, but a lot of my more stringent objections are about how you're portraying the reality of violence (as in the real world) rather than how one chooses to abstract that into the game; which I don't feel is a simple matter of opinions. </div> </div> </div>Well, the Bleeding Tilt <i>might</i> be the only way to represent the reality of possibly dying from one shot. So, I will certainly use it, most likely in lieu of increasing damage. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is a rather frustrating statement. The average damage is not a cap on damage. Averaging 3-5 damage does not mean you will not roll more than 5 total damage ever. It's an average, <b>rolls will perform above and below average.</b> </div> </div> </div>Which is why I use an average result. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A character with 4 dice, spending a WP, and using a 2 damage gun, has a &gt;7.5% chance to do 7 <b>or more</b> damage. That means they'll be dealing lethal attacks against average Health characters more frequently than you'd roll a natural 20 in D&amp;D. That means if five cops are having a shoot out with five gang members who all have these stats, there's ~83% one of them dies (or at least gets KOed and is dying) the first round of shooting (and the second round is going to get really messy with cumulative damage building up).<br /> <br /> So please, be careful with how to look at these numbers. Something like a 5% chance of a one-hit kill might feel very small per attack, but in a sub-system like combat with a lot of rolls, a 5% odds event will statistically come up fairly frequently even though it is such an outlier on an individual roll. </div> </div> </div>You make a good point here, which is why I'll probably not increase damage. Buth when I'm looking at this example I get split down the middle. On one hand, I agree with you and see the logic of what you're saying. Then the other side rears it's ugly head: it's taking a 7 dice pool and a 2 damage gun to get a 5% chance to <i><b>maybe</b></i> kill a person from a gunshot wound?? Real world statistics puts that chance three times higher.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How many of those situations should be full combat instead of Down and Dirty combat? There's a reason the game doesn't force all violence into the full combat system. Sometimes that's not the best subsystem for the situation. How many of these should even be rolled? The CofD systems are expected to be selected as what's most appropriate to the situation. </div> </div> </div>True. This is reminiscent of many a &quot;Rule Zero&quot; conversations I've heard/read. But I understand that not all violent encounters should be played through full combat.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why would I ever roll an NPC child shooting another NPC child while playing around with an unsecured gun? </div> </div> </div>You wouldn't. Neither would I. I only mention it to illustrate my point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why would I treat a spouse killing a cheating partner by surprise as a full combat situation instead of a Down and Dirty combat situation if it seems important enough to roll out? Either they kill their partner or they don't. That's the drama of the situation.<br /> <br /> The combat rules are not the &quot;physics&quot; of the game. </div> </div> </div>I see your point. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes. The point here though, is that these are in reality rather rare, not that they don't happen, and in the rules-as-is, this is reflected by having outright kills be mathematically rare compared to multiple attack kills or mortal wounds that kill over time. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But this is what I was trying to get at. What makes you say this? Because the rules do allow for it. With potentially infinitely exploding dice, no number of successes on a roll is impossible, just growingly improbable. As well, the rules do not force all violence into the full combat system to mitigate some of the difficulty of the combat system with dramatic situations that aren't focusing on duking it out. How does &quot;unlikely&quot; get to &quot;impossible&quot; for you in this? </div> </div> </div>True, they are rare. But they're not 5% (or lower) rare. The math in the rules makes dying from one bullet much more rare than in the real world...which finally circles back to my original point. But, again, I can certainly use additional rules to make combat more lethal than standard combat is currently.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T03:42:12-07:00","id":1099787,"title":"","content":"Do also bear in mind that CofD is a <i>game</i> system as well. One hit kills are often not very conductive to fun play (depending on what you are aiming for), so some degree of survivability is likely a design goal. Bleeding out, rather than insta-death, supports this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T05:04:21-07:00","id":1099792,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099787#post1099787\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do also bear in mind that CofD is a <i>game</i> system as well. One hit kills are often not very conductive to fun play (depending on what you are aiming for), so some degree of survivability is likely a design goal. Bleeding out, rather than insta-death, supports this.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, sir. That is indeed a valid point, and one that I keep coming back to as well. I was initially unsure of the balance between how well the system's verisimilitude and it existence as a game system. I am a bit more sure after this conversation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12124-lord-dynel","name":"Lord Dynel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12124_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T05:31:18-07:00","id":1099794,"title":"","content":"I personally feel that this combat system enables following:<br /> 1. To achieve victory or defeat is relatively easy; it's not that hard to inflict a combat-tilting tilt, or to bring superior numbers, and so on.<br /> 2. To *kill* the targets, in one go, is harder. <br /> 3. There is almost always a window for the defeated party to run away - because instant kills are so rare, you will usually have at least one round available where you can make a choice of &quot;get me out of here&quot;<br /> <br /> Looking at the fact that this is a horror game where default mortals probably should be brutalized by the horrors but escape by the skin of their teeth, I think this is working properly.<br /> Note that by increasing gun lethality, you are giving more power to mortals, as usually they are the ones with guns while monsters are the ones with claws and large health pools.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T15:03:21-07:00","id":1099927,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Dynel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1094247-implications-of-increasing-damage?p=1099705#post1099705\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well...a more lethal gun would certainly appeal to realism... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>I think we're at a bit of an impasse on this. Maybe linking to some of the things you're reading? Because I'm not seeing anything that says 25-30% instant fatality rates from gunshots. Everything I can find that gets up to that level includes what would be represented in the system with either the Bleeding Tilt or the natural upgrading of damage if you're full on Lethal.<br /> <br /> Some of this might be that &quot;dead&quot; is actually a very medically complicated thing. We don't have the best definition of when someone is actually dead. There's plenty of cases where a patient arrives at an ER with signs of life, but is long past the chance of revival. Some people call that dead (unconscious, non-responsive, major organ failure), and others don't, because the brain's still active or heart is still pumping.<br /> <br /> In general though, I'd argue that the realism issue in the game isn't the rules of guns. It's the rules on Health. The Health rules are exceedingly unrealistic (mostly because realistic health rules would be extremely unfun for mortal games as cumulative long term disabilities start wracking up any time damage starts to come into the game).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which is why I use an average result. </div> </div> </div>But that's the problem. Treating &quot;average result&quot; as &quot;what will happen&quot; is not how averages are meant to work.<br /> <br /> The average of {1,1,1,1,96} is 20. But not only are you never going to get 20, you have an 80% chance of getting a 1. The average of 20 is only going to emerge out of an exceedingly large number of random draws.<br /> <br /> This is why for simple sets like the above we look at mead, median, and mode (20, 1 and 1 respectively for the example, which tells you a lot when you look at all three 'averages'), and in more complicated random systems (like the CofD dice mechanics) we look at things like variance and deviation. Just figuring out the average is not enough to predict what the spread of rolls will look like in application.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is reminiscent of many a &quot;Rule Zero&quot; conversations I've heard/read. </div> </div> </div>The significant difference here is that &quot;Rule Zero&quot; is brought up in regards to the idea that broken rule is still broken even if you use the Rule Zero to fix it; fixing it is in fact acknowledging it is broken.<br /> <br /> In this case, we're not talking about the system being &quot;broken&quot; but built on the assumption that the ST will select subsystems appropriate to the situation.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}